WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.420
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.153
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.453 --> 00:00:16.819
Unit fifty-eight: Environmental Law. In this unit, you will discuss how laws protect the environment.

4
00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.919
environmental impact assessment.

5
00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:27.972
environmental impact assessment means a study of how a project will affect the environment.

6
00:00:28.272 --> 00:00:32.733
Every large dam requires an environmental impact assessment.

7
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:37.955
Your turn. Say: environmental impact assessment.

8
00:00:40.655 --> 00:00:43.873
environmental impact assessment.

9
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:46.198
conservation law.

10
00:00:46.898 --> 00:00:51.208
conservation law means legislation that protects nature and wildlife.

11
00:00:51.508 --> 00:00:55.075
The new conservation law expanded protected forests.

12
00:00:55.775 --> 00:00:58.950
Your turn. Say: conservation law.

13
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.929
conservation law.

14
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:05.463
protected species.

15
00:01:06.163 --> 00:01:10.891
protected species means animals or plants that the law forbids harming.

16
00:01:11.191 --> 00:01:14.120
The Irrawaddy dolphin is a protected species.

17
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.238
Your turn. Say: protected species.

18
00:01:20.938 --> 00:01:22.485
protected species.

19
00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:24.647
illegal logging.

20
00:01:25.347 --> 00:01:29.297
illegal logging means cutting down trees without legal permission.

21
00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:32.955
Illegal logging threatens Cambodia's forests.

22
00:01:33.655 --> 00:01:37.027
Your turn. Say: illegal logging.

23
00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:40.926
illegal logging.

24
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:43.533
wildlife trafficking.

25
00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:48.787
wildlife trafficking means illegal trade in wild animals or their parts.

26
00:01:49.087 --> 00:01:52.410
Wildlife trafficking is a multi-billion-dollar crime.

27
00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.796
Your turn. Say: wildlife trafficking.

28
00:01:59.496 --> 00:02:01.031
wildlife trafficking.

29
00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:04.144
environmental court.

30
00:02:04.844 --> 00:02:10.756
environmental court means a court that specialises in environmental cases.

31
00:02:11.056 --> 00:02:14.994
Some countries have created environmental courts.

32
00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:19.821
Your turn. Say: environmental court.

33
00:02:22.521 --> 00:02:24.765
environmental court.

34
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.099
pollution permit.

35
00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:32.144
pollution permit means official permission to release limited pollution.

36
00:02:32.444 --> 00:02:35.222
The factory exceeded its pollution permit.

37
00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:39.178
Your turn. Say: pollution permit.

38
00:02:41.878 --> 00:02:43.064
pollution permit.

39
00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.504
enforcement gap.

40
00:02:46.204 --> 00:02:52.197
enforcement gap means the difference between laws on paper and how they are applied.

41
00:02:52.497 --> 00:02:55.936
The enforcement gap allows illegal activity to continue.

42
00:02:56.636 --> 00:02:59.765
Your turn. Say: enforcement gap.

43
00:03:02.465 --> 00:03:03.930
enforcement gap.

44
00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.997
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:07.297 --> 00:03:12.141
Ms. Sarah, Cambodia has strong conservation laws on paper, doesn't it?

46
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:17.005
Yes, but the enforcement gap is the real challenge.

47
00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:20.641
Why do illegal loggers get away with it?

48
00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:27.830
Remote areas, limited rangers, and sometimes corruption. Rangers can't keep up.

49
00:03:29.117 --> 00:03:31.000
What about wildlife trafficking?

50
00:03:32.317 --> 00:03:37.465
International networks are behind it. Countries need to crack down together.

51
00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.617
Should every big project go through an environmental impact assessment?

52
00:03:44.917 --> 00:03:49.679
Absolutely, and communities should be able to speak out if the assessment is weak.

53
00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:54.748
Grammar focus: Advanced phrasal verbs (mastery).

54
00:03:55.048 --> 00:03:58.243
Advanced phrasal verbs are common in news and debate.

55
00:03:58.543 --> 00:04:04.908
Loggers get away with it; countries must crack down on trafficking; communities should speak out.

56
00:04:05.208 --> 00:04:07.718
Know their formal equivalents for writing.

57
00:04:08.018 --> 00:04:15.498
In formal writing, replace 'crack down on' with 'take strict action against' and 'get away with' with 'avoid punishment for'.

58
00:04:16.483 --> 00:04:19.751
Now read and listen. Laws That Protect Nature.

59
00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:24.976
Environmental laws have expanded rapidly over the past fifty years.

60
00:04:26.283 --> 00:04:32.068
Most countries now require an environmental impact assessment before major construction.

61
00:04:33.383 --> 00:04:38.262
Conservation laws protect forests, rivers and endangered wildlife.

62
00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:44.394
In Cambodia, the Irrawaddy dolphin in the Mekong is a protected species.

63
00:04:45.683 --> 00:04:50.237
However, illegal logging and wildlife trafficking continue in many regions.

64
00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:56.255
The main problem is often the enforcement gap rather than the absence of laws.

65
00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:02.777
Some countries have created environmental courts to handle cases more effectively.

66
00:05:04.083 --> 00:05:10.332
Ultimately, laws work best when local communities are empowered to protect their own environment.

67
00:05:11.617 --> 00:05:14.048
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:16.928
Cutting down trees without permission is blank.

69
00:05:17.228 --> 00:05:22.972
A, illegal logging. B, wildlife trafficking. C, pollution permit.

70
00:05:26.983 --> 00:05:30.005
The answer is A: illegal logging.

71
00:05:31.517 --> 00:05:34.654
The police promised to crack blank on illegal parking.

72
00:05:34.954 --> 00:05:37.934
A, up. B, down. C, out.

73
00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.008
The answer is B: down.

74
00:05:45.517 --> 00:05:47.551
He thought he could get blank with cheating.

75
00:05:47.851 --> 00:05:50.564
A, away. B, off. C, out.

76
00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:56.700
The answer is A: away.

77
00:05:58.217 --> 00:06:02.399
The difference between laws on paper and practice is the blank.

78
00:06:02.699 --> 00:06:08.744
A, enforcement gap. B, environmental court. C, conservation law.

79
00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.924
The answer is A: enforcement gap.

80
00:06:16.417 --> 00:06:19.984
According to the text, what is often the main problem?

81
00:06:20.284 --> 00:06:26.154
A, no laws. B, the enforcement gap. C, too many courts.

82
00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.148
The answer is B: the enforcement gap.

83
00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:41.064
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can environmental laws be enforced more effectively?

84
00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:52.264
Part two: Use two phrasal verbs, propose two measures, and explain one obstacle.

85
00:06:58.564 --> 00:07:12.504
Governments should crack down on trafficking networks and train more rangers. Communities should also be able to speak out safely. The main obstacle is that remote areas make it easy for offenders to get away with illegal activity.

86
00:07:12.804 --> 00:07:15.013
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:16.017 --> 00:07:21.212
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced phrasal verbs (mastery).

88
00:07:21.512 --> 00:07:26.602
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

89
00:07:26.902 --> 00:07:29.856
See you in Unit fifty-nine. Goodbye!

90
00:07:30.156 --> 00:07:33.122
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

